    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Eisenkot quickly targets Netanyahu’s legacy in new Israeli parliament",
    summary: "Though questions still linger over who can form a government, Gadi Eisenkot's broad left-to-right bloc wasted no time in setting the agenda.",
    preview: "Jerusalem – Israeli ex-army chief Gadi Eisenkot and his allies launched a legislative onslaught against caretaker Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s legacy on Tuesday, as a new parliament convened in a heated first session. Though questions still linger over who can form a government, Eisenkot’s broad left-to-right bloc wasted no time in setting the Knesset’s agenda with a green light from Arab-Israeli lawmakers, whose role has stirred up heated opposition from Netanyahu’s base and even right-leaning supporters of Eisenkot.",
    body: "Jerusalem – Israeli ex-army chief Gadi Eisenkot and his allies launched a legislative onslaught against caretaker Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s legacy on Tuesday, as a new parliament convened in a heated first session.\n\nThough questions still linger over who can form a government, Eisenkot’s broad left-to-right bloc wasted no time in setting the Knesset’s agenda with a green light from Arab-Israeli lawmakers, whose role has stirred up heated opposition from Netanyahu’s base and even right-leaning supporters of Eisenkot.\n\nEisenkot’s Yashar party said it submitted a slew of proposals before the new parliament, including establishing an independent commission to investigate security failures that allowed the Hamas attack on Oct. 7, 2023 — which Netanyahu has long opposed.",
