    id: "0d17733e2c16e52d7ca2",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Russia expels Moldovan diplomat in tit-for-tat move",
    summary: "Relations between Moscow and Chisinau have worsened since Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022.",
    preview: "Relations between Moscow and Chisinau have worsened since the 2022 Ukraine war. Russia has expelled a Moldovan diplomat in retaliation for Chisinau’s expulsion of a Russian embassy employee over the crash of a drone in Moldova. Russia’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs said on Tuesday that it had summoned Moldova’s ambassador, Lilian Darii, and informed him that a member of the Moldovan embassy’s staff had been declared unacceptable “on the basis of the principle of reciprocity”.",
    body: "Relations between Moscow and Chisinau have worsened since the 2022 Ukraine war.\n\nRussia has expelled a Moldovan diplomat in retaliation for Chisinau’s expulsion of a Russian embassy employee over the crash of a drone in Moldova.\n\nRussia’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs said on Tuesday that it had summoned Moldova’s ambassador, Lilian Darii, and informed him that a member of the Moldovan embassy’s staff had been declared unacceptable “on the basis of the principle of reciprocity”.\n\n“A strong protest was lodged with the ambassador after the Moldovan authorities on October 23 declared an employee of the Russian embassy in Chisinau persona non grata,” the ministry said in a statement.\n\nIt said Moldova’s decision was made “under the formal pretext of the alleged discovery on the territory of the republic of debris of an unmanned aerial vehicle carrying explosives”, calling it “another unfriendly step”.\n\nThe ministry said the “true goal” of the move was “to further undermine the centuries-long history of Russian-Moldovan relations”.\n\n‘Crashed near homes’\n\nLast month, Moldovan President Maia Sandu said a “Russian attack drone” had violated the country’s airspace and “crashed near homes and an energy facility”.\n\nMoldova then declared a staff member of the Russian embassy “persona non grata”.\n\nDrones have penetrated Moldovan territory on multiple occasions since Russia launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\n\nSince then, Russia’s relations with former Soviet states, such as Moldova, have hit an all-time low, compounded by the push by some of those states to join the European Union.\n\nOn Sunday, Moldova officially launched a new power line connecting the country to neighbouring Romania and the European grid to reduce its dependence on Russian energy.\n\nThe 400-kilovolt extra-high-voltage line, which runs from the city of Vulcanesti to Chisinau, allows Moldova to cut reliance on the Cuciurgan thermal power plant in Transnistria.\n\nOther European-aligned states in the Black Sea region say they are experiencing renewed attacks allegedly carried out by Moscow, such as a drone strike that sank a cargo ship off Romania’s Black Sea coast in October.",
